.TH CVEC_PUSH 3 "2026-09-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_push \- push an element onto the vector (type-inferred)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_push(v, new_elem);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro is a type-inferred wrapper for cvector_push_back(). It converts
new_elem to the declared element type of v with an ordinary C assignment,
exactly as "T tmp = new_elem;" does, and then copies that converted value
into the vector, so the pushed value is the result of a plain C
assignment and not the raw byte representation of new_elem. The macro
calls ccol_fatal_err() when cvector_push_back() fails.
For an element type that is an array, such as char[64], new_elem is an
array or a string literal: the macro zero-fills the element and copies the
bytes of new_elem into it, up to the smaller of the two sizes. A pointer
as new_elem for such a type is a compile error, because the macro cannot
see the size of what it points at.
The macro never takes the address of new_elem; it first copies the value
into a temporary of the element type. A literal, a calculated expression
or a struct value that a function returns is therefore as correct as a
variable.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v         Vector to push onto
new_elem  The element. It can be a variable, a literal or any
          expression, for example a struct value that a function
          returns
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program on failure.
The macro converts the value of new_elem to the declared element type of
v in the same way as a plain C assignment. For example, an int literal
that goes into a cvec_construct(v, long) becomes a long value, and a
float that goes into a cvec_construct(v, int) truncates to an int, in the
same way as "int x = some_float;". The macro never copies the raw bit
pattern of new_elem into an element of a different type, even when the
two types have the same size.
A new_elem of a type that has no implicit conversion to the declared
element type of v is a compile error at this point; the macro never
reinterprets such a value silently.
new_elem can safely alias into the backing buffer of v, for example when
it is cvec_at(v, i). The macro reads the value into a private
temporary that does not alias before it touches v, so a capacity growth
that this call starts can never invalidate the value.
With GCC 11 or later, the macro sets every padding byte of its temporary
to zero before it pushes it, so a struct element is stored with zero
padding, which cvec_find(3) relies on. With a compiler that offers no
__builtin_clear_padding, such as Clang, the padding bytes of the stored
element are unspecified.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cvec_construct(vec, int);
int x = 42;
cvec_push(vec, x);
cvec_destroy(vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvector_push_back (3)
